There is the bug: .char-tile-name is display flex with width 148.5 and height 25, but the epithet is at y466 while style starts at y455.8, overlapping! Why? `.char-tile-style` has margin-top 9 but starts at 455.8 while epithet bottom 476.8. Because `.char-tile-name` is `display:flex` but name's height 25 and style's y=455.8? Let's see: content is bottom anchored (bottom 11px) with height auto. Since content is abs positioned with top auto/bottom 11, its height is the sum of children, but name has height 25, epithet height 10 with margin-top 5, style height 60 with margin-top 9, lock 10 with margin-top 10. Total = 25+5+10+9+60+10+10 = 129. Tile bottom? content y? Truncated tile info. If content height should be 129 and bottom 11, top = tile bottom - 11 - 129. Yet children's y suggests style overlaps with epithet because of negative? Wait style y 455.8 is *above* epithet y 466.8. That means style is being positioned before the epithet? Impossible in normal flow unless margins collapse or the spans are absolutely positioned. Maybe `.char-tile-name` includes the epithet? No.

Hold on, the query selector `.char-tile` first could be a title-card? No, char-tile only. `.char-tile-name` is a span, `.char-tile-epithet` is a span, `.char-tile-style` is a span. All are inline elements by default, but `.char-tile-name` display flex (block-level), `.char-tile-epithet` default inline, `.char-tile-style` default inline. Inline elements with vertical margins don't affect line box and can overlap? Actually inline spans sit on the same line unless block. `.char-tile-epithet` is inline and `.char-tile-style` is inline, so both flow inline after the block-level `.char-tile-name` line, sharing a line box. That's the overlap: epithet and style are inline, creating a single line where style's padding/border etc. overlaps. The CSS sets `.char-tile-style` margin-top but inline vertical margins don't apply; `display` not set to block. On desktop, maybe because content width larger, they still overlap? Let's check desktop select screenshot? We didn't take. Likely broken there too. The `<span>`s need `display: block` for epithet and style (and lock already inline-flex). The style span child has display block. So fix:

```
.char-tile-epithet { display:block; }
.char-tile-style { display:block; }
```

That resolves overlap. Also maybe char-tile-lock is inline-flex, fine.

